print(dates)                                  # [''01'', ''02'']
print(DATE.pattern[:6], DATE.groups)          # (?P<y> 3
Key point
re caches recently used patterns, so re.compile is mostly about readability and reuse — a named, documented constant at module level.
Practical recipes
import re
EMAIL = re.compile(r"[\w.+-]+@[\w-]+(\.[\w-]+)+")   # good enough for forms, not RFC 5322
print(bool(EMAIL.fullmatch("a.b+tag@mail.co.in")), bool(EMAIL.fullmatch("a@b")))   # True False

tokens = re.findall(r"\d+\.?\d*|[-+*/()]", "3.5*(2+10)/4")
print(tokens)          # [''3.5'', ''*'', ''('', ''2'', ''+'', ''10'', '')'', ''/'', ''4'']

def snake(name):
    return re.sub(r"(?<!^)(?=[A-Z])", "_", name).lower()
print(snake("parseHTTPResponseCode"))       # parse_h_t_t_p_response_code
print(re.sub(r"([a-z0-9])([A-Z])", r"\1_\2", "userIdCount").lower())   # user_id_count

print(re.findall(r"#(\w+)", "loving #python and #regex!"))   # [''python'', ''regex'']
Performance & when not to use regex
ReDoS
Nested quantifiers like (a+)+$ or (\w+\s?)*$ can cause catastrophic backtracking: on a near-miss input the engine tries exponentially many paths and hangs (a ReDoS vulnerability). Avoid ambiguous nested repetition; prefer s'), 'C'),
