Why does printf use %f for both float and double, but scanf needs %lf for double?
Arguments to variadic functions undergo default argument promotions: a float passed to printf is converted to double, so %f always receives a double. scanf receives pointers, which are not promoted — it must know whether to write 4 bytes (float *, %f) or 8 bytes (double *, %lf).'), 'C'),
  TIMESTAMPTZ '2026-10-07 03:23:53+00'
