Friend class
#include <iostream>

class Engine {
    int rpm_ = 0;
    friend class Mechanic;          // Mechanic may touch Engine''s privates
};

class Mechanic {
public:
    static void tune(Engine& e, int rpm) { e.rpm_ = rpm; }
    static int read(const Engine& e) { return e.rpm_; }
};

int main() {
    Engine e;
    Mechanic::tune(e, 3000);
    std::cout << Mechanic::read(e) << ''\n'';
}
3000
Friendship is granted, not taken — the class decides who its friends are.
It''s not symmetric (A friend of B doesn''t make B a friend of A), not transitive, and not inherited.
A hidden friend (defined inside the class) is found only by argument-dependent lookup — the preferred way to write operators.
Do
Use friends for tightly coupled pairs: operators, stream I/O, a container and its iterator, test helpers.
Avoid
Using friend to dodge a design problem — if many outsiders need private access, the interface is wrong.
Does friend break encapsulation?
Used deliberately, it strengthens it: instead of adding public getters/setters that everyone can call, you grant access to one specific function that is logically part of the class''s interface (like operator<<). The class still controls exactly who gets in.'), 'C'),
