SELECT 'f6435e73-a619-761b-81b2-60fe7aa6e314', '629e313d-9fab-7a8a-9371-9b17d20cafe2', 'Inheritance & Access Specifiers', 'Inheritance & Access Specifiers', 'A derived class inherits the members of its base class and can add or customise behaviour. Public inheritance models an is-a relationship.
Basics
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Animal {
public:
    explicit Animal(std::string name) : name_(std::move(name)) { std::cout << "Animal()\n"; }
    ~Animal() { std::cout << "~Animal()\n"; }
    void eat() const { std::cout << name_ << " eats\n"; }
protected:
    std::string name_;                 // visible to derived classes, not to outsiders
};

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    Dog(std::string name, std::string breed)
        : Animal(std::move(name)), breed_(std::move(breed)) { std::cout << "Dog()\n"; }   // base first
    ~Dog() { std::cout << "~Dog()\n"; }
    void bark() const { std::cout << name_ << " the " << breed_ << " barks\n"; }
private:
    std::string breed_;
};

int main() {
    Dog d("Rex", "beagle");
    d.eat();          // inherited
    d.bark();         // added
}
Animal()
Dog()
Rex eats
Rex the beagle barks
~Dog()
~Animal()
Order
Construction goes base → members → derived body; destruction runs in exactly the reverse order.
Inheritance access
Base member is…	public inheritance	protected inheritance	private inheritance
public	public	protected	private
protected	protected	protected	private
private	inaccessible	inaccessible	inaccessible
public inheritance = is-a (a Dog can be used wherever an Animal& is expected). 99% of the time this is what you want.
private inheritance = implemented-in-terms-of — usually better expressed with composition (a member).
class D : B defaults to private inheritance; struct D : B to public.
Hiding, using and calling the base version
#include <iostream>

struct Base {
    void greet() const { std::cout << "Base::greet\n"; }
    void greet(int n) const { std::cout << "Base::greet(" << n << ")\n"; }
};

struct Derived : Base {
    using Base::greet;                 // without this, greet(int) would be hidden
    void greet() const {
        std::cout << "Derived::greet, then ";
        Base::greet();                 // call the base version explicitly
    }
};

struct WithCtors : Base {
    using Base::Base;                  // inherit constructors (C++11)
};

int main() {
    Derived d;
    d.greet();
    d.greet(3);
}
Derived::greet, then Base::greet
Base::greet(3)
Watch out
Declaring a function in the derived class with the same name hides all base overloads of that name, whatever their parameters. Bring them back with using Base::name;.
Composition vs inheritance
Do
Use inheritance for genuine is-a relationships with polymorphic behaviour; prefer composition (has-a) for reuse: a Car has an Engine.
Avoid
Inheriting just to reuse code (class Stack : public std::vector<int>) — it exposes every base operation and couples you to its implementation.
What is not inherited from a base class?
Constructors (unless brought in with using Base::Base;), the destructor, copy/move assignment operators (the derived class gets its own implicitly-declared ones), and friendships. Private members are inherited — they''re part of the object — but not accessible.',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Inheritance & Access Specifiers'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Inheritance & Access Specifiers'), 'B')
